(4390356, 629740, 39850, '<td valign="top">Shaking my head a little at this (DQ) - but I guess there were many teething issues and lessons learned from the new rules.  Maybe this Challenge should have been made triptych, or whatever ''tych''.  Time lapse is <i>usually</i> a set of images that move to show the time lapse, at least that was my understanding.  Trying to capture multiple frames in the one image, especially for a scene like this (where I presume the subject stayed in the one position) is near impossible.  You must have been disappointed, but (and I did stumble across your post in the thread), at least the thrill of seeing this holds strong in your memory.  Good capture(s) and an interesting image.</td>', 'Shaking my head a little at this (DQ) - but I guess there were many teething issues and lessons learned from the new rules.  Maybe this Challenge should have been made triptych, or whatever ''tych''.  Time lapse is usually a set of images that move to show the time lapse, at least that was my understanding.  Trying to capture multiple frames in the one image, especially for a scene like this (where I presume the subject stayed in the one position) is near impossible.  You must have been disappointed, but (and I did stumble across your post in the thread), at least the thrill of seeing this holds strong in your memory.  Good capture(s) and an interesting image.', '2008-01-14 00:29:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(4390360, 629583, 41337, '<td valign="top">Congrats. You almost beat Falc! The masking isn''t too bad but I wonder how you resized it. I find that using bicubic smoother is better for downsizing if there is a lot of thin lines you want to preserve like that golf club shaft as it doesn''t make the lines so pixelated. Or better yet use both bicubic sharper and smoother and then mask the two together so that you get the best of both worlds. Anyway just a thought.</td>', 'Congrats. You almost beat Falc! The masking isn''t too bad but I wonder how you resized it. I find that using bicubic smoother is better for downsizing if there is a lot of thin lines you want to preserve like that golf club shaft as it doesn''t make the lines so pixelated. Or better yet use both bicubic sharper and smoother and then mask the two together so that you get the best of both worlds. Anyway just a thought.', '2008-01-14 00:31:42.000000', NULL, 0),
